    "q-335373": {
      "dom_id": "step-q335373",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)+2x(t)+y(t)-1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)+3y(t)-{y}^{2}(t)-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. An equilibrium point of the system is $[0,\\,1]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting this equilibrium to the origin?",
      "worked_solution": "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and $u=[\\begin{aligned}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,x=u+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t)=u(t),\\,y(t)=v(t)+1,\\,{x}^{′}(t)={u}^{′}(t),\\,{v}^{′}(t)={y}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)+2x(t)+y(t)-1 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & ={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+(v(t)+1)-1 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =-2x(t)+3y(t)-{y}^{2}(t)-2 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =-2u(t)+3(v(t)+1)-(v(t)+1)^{2}-2 \\\\  & =-2u(t)+3v(t)+3-({v}^{2}(t)+2v(t)+1)-2 \\\\  & =-2u(t)+v(t)-{v}^{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=-2u(t)+v(t)-{v}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
